Title: Upstream concurrency floor: busy_timeout and the cross-library wipe guard
Created: 2026-08-26
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-08-26T15:43Z claude created

2026-08-26T16:53Z claude note PR-2 rescoped to busy_timeout alone per the ratified re-form; the commit-cadence half moved to scoped issue C (0035)
--- body ---
## Context
DESIGN.md §4 PR-2 and PR-3, honoring R13 and R12. Verified at edf2748: zero
busy_timeout or SQLITE_BUSY handling anywhere in src/; empirically reproduced
this cycle — two handles on one db, default timeout 0, the second handle's
first write throws "database is locked" in 0 ms. And the build path reaches
clearStore() (build.ts), so a build for one library wipes another's index —
the R12 hazard DESIGN-DELTA cites.
PR-2: busy_timeout=5000 on every connection + per-page commits on the BUILD
path only — explicitly preserving the update path's single-transaction
rollback (upstream's comment is right: a half-applied delta is a wrong index).
PR-3: a build for a different library than stamped refuses with a notice
naming both, instead of clearStore(). The guard, not the multi-library feature.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] PR-2: two-handle SQLITE_BUSY repro as the failing test; update rollback untouched
- [ ] PR-3: cross-library refusal with both libraries named; clearStore chain as repro
- [ ] both PRs opened upstream; outcomes recorded here

## Amendments (implementation review, 2026-08-26)
PR-2: the persist cadence already exists (index-manager.ts:472-473, 200
items/10 s) BUT crawlOptions overrides it to 500 items/60 s on every fulltext
build (build.ts:321) — the longest-held lock on the path that matters. The PR
must lower that override too, or its own repro still fails under fulltext.
PR-3 rescoped: no library identity is persisted today, and build.ts:185-197
deliberately avoids keying by routed library id (users/0 locally aliases
users/<id> on cloud). The PR must (a) stamp a canonical {type,id} with the
personal library normalized to one token, then (b) guard on it — and must not
refuse legitimate rebuilds across the local/cloud seam. Quote his doc-comment
in the body and frame the guard as ENFORCING the documented single-library
assumption (political review, finding 4).
